drills.explanation:
Ask the questions in order, each one able to stop the retry:

```java
boolean safe = failure.kind().equals("connect-timeout") || IDEMPOTENT.contains(call.method()) || call.hasIdempotencyKey();
if (!safe) return Decision.stop("not idempotent");
boolean useful = failure.kind().endsWith("timeout") || RETRYABLE.contains(failure.status());
if (!useful) return Decision.stop("not retryable");
if (attempt >= maxAttempts) return Decision.stop("attempts exhausted");
long delay = serverSays != null ? Math.min(serverSays, 30_000) : jitter.applyAsLong(Math.min(100L << (attempt - 1), 5_000));
return delay >= remainingMs ? Decision.stop("deadline") : Decision.retryAfter(delay);
```

The tests catch the plausible wrong policies. Retrying any 503 regardless of method repeats a POST that may already have charged (the POST test expects "not idempotent"). Treating every timeout as safe repeats a POST whose request was sent (the request-timeout test), while refusing a connect timeout throws away a retry that cannot duplicate anything. Retrying every 5xx and 4xx retries 404 and 500 (the 4xx test). Ignoring `Retry-After` waits 100 ms when the server asked for 2 s, and forgetting the cap of 5,000 ms on the ceiling makes attempt 10 wait 51 s (the boundary test).

