drills.explanation:
Work out the remaining budget from the injected clock, refuse early, then build the request with the smaller timeout:

```java
Duration remaining = Duration.between(clock.instant(), deadline);
Duration usable = remaining.minus(RESERVE);
if (usable.compareTo(MIN_USEFUL) < 0) throw new DeadlineExceeded("only " + remaining.toMillis() + " ms left");
Duration timeout = usable.compareTo(perCallCap) < 0 ? usable : perCallCap;
return HttpRequest.newBuilder(uri).GET().timeout(timeout).header("X-Deadline", deadline.toString()).build();
```

The tests read `timeout()` and `connectTimeout()` back from the JDK objects, so no network is involved. Always using `perCallCap` outlives a caller with 300 ms left (the tight-budget test expects 280 ms). Forgetting the reserve gives 300 ms, leaving no time to build the answer. Comparing `remaining` instead of `usable` with the minimum lets a call with 60 ms left through (the refusal test). Calling `Instant.now()` instead of the injected clock makes every expectation depend on when the test runs.

